#{[17592186262758 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262758 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262758 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262758 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262758 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "235350dc-4663-567f-92d6-1215c91e7efd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262758 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/3a/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\,dy \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262759 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262759 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262759 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262759 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d93ec47e-8065-5376-b8dd-7b5786ea175a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262759 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/34/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262760 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262760 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262760 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262760 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,{x}^{3}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262760 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a994fc8-218e-56c0-8e85-b472da8df87e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262760 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/006d80a7dbe4d03b78cc5fb1a642405e3fdfdd69ec9d80014ee8e0355ad1ae73.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{y/2}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[x]_{-y/2}^{y/2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[\\frac{y}{2}-(-\\frac{y}{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186387807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387804 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a7357b1f-7672-551e-bf04-066c16895432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387805 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a2f6f5b-f063-50fd-a445-e5206861484d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387806 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387806 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387806 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ca3a5b14-e539-50ca-b764-e8483d96d0a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186387807 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17