ef 17592186481143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481334 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f1420a1f-e109-58bc-a6f7-ed1ec23a0ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481444 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481444 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1736de57-90cb-505e-97a3-07df8bb92125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481444 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481472 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481472 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6fdd744-d51d-5e7d-83a1-1dc38e3a78f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481472 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186262736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481473 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481473 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7a03c0e-bfb8-569b-b911-e9045812e018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481473 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186387803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481474 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec17ec80-3152-54be-96ed-dd7762345db6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481474 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186481405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520740 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520738 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186520740 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186520740 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y},\\,4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186520740 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25491ab2-3faf-524d-80c3-eb7b98861b8b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186520740 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=8 x y,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=8 x y.\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}s &=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(16 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x^{2} y\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(24 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\left[12 y^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186520745 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186172101.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186520745 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "807bda25-3871-5a18-ad95-5158c9246bea" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540642 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540642 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540642 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42f7866a-874a-59e8-98df-e82497daf28c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540644 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540644 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540644 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "44289665-fb34-50eb-942c-684dbafac462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540685 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540685 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540685 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d52696f-f66a-5483-9d31-ad3010ca9bf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540704 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540704 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540704 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "675393a7-a22d-52c0-8122-89d84461848c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540734 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540734 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540734 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b1a18cf-57e7-594a-bab2-ece7b5646c56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541040 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dcc96fea-c51a-5b5e-9f54-45e9c86734fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541081 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3},\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "512baac6-6af2-5983-be4f-040d5e0743f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541081 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}y+x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[((1)^{3}y+1)-((-1)^{3}y+(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}+2y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =((2)^{2}+2(2))-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
